   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Adjectives before the noun",
     "texto": [
      "Korean adjectives take a special form before a noun: **-(으)ㄴ**. With a stem ending in a vowel, **-ㄴ** is usually added; with another consonant, **-은**: 크다 → **큰 집** (big house), 작다 → **작은 방** (small room), 좋다 → **좋은 사람** (good person). Do not confuse this form with the past-tense form of verbs."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Common cases",
     "texto": [
      "Stems ending in **ㄹ** lose that consonant: 길다 → **긴 머리**. Adjectives ending in **-하다** usually form **-한** (친절하다 → 친절한 사람); some adjectives ending in **ㅡ** contract the vowel when the ending is added: 예쁘다 → 예쁜 옷, 크다 → 큰 집. The form goes directly before the noun: **집이 커요** (the house is big), but **큰 집** (big house)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
